as an accent can be set with the argument $accent-rule. Some accent rule cases can be selected simply $with the menu of that argument. For example, :longer-than-$predecessor specifies that any note that is longer than its $preceding note and not shorter than the following note is $considered an accented note. $$The argument strictness controls how such accents are $constrained. When set to :note, any accented note occurs $on a metric position specified by the argument metric $structure (either the first beat of a bar or any beat). $However, also unaccented notes can occur on such metric $positions. When the strictness is set to :position, then every $metric position specified by the argument metric structure $will carry an accented note. However, such accents can also $occur between such metric positions.  $$There does not exist general agreement on what defines an $accent. Therefore, accent rules can also be given as argument $instead of using the menu, and such accent rules can even $support arguments themselves. The box $mk-accent-has-at-least-duration-ar returns a rather simple $accent rule: any note beyond the specified duration is $considered an accent. $$BUG: in metric-accents: the first and last two notes may $not follow this rule.") "" (om-make-point 572 241) nil (om-make-color 0 0 0) (om-make-font "Verdana" 12 :family "Verdana" :style (quote (:plain)) :mode (quote nil))))) (when (fboundp (quote set-active)) (set-active box nil)) box) (let ((box (om-load-editor-box1 "poly" (quote poly) (quote ((om-load-inputfun (quote input-funbox) "object" "self" nil) (om-load-inputfun (quote input-funbox) "list of VOICE objects" "voices" (list (let ((newobj (when (find-class (quote voice) nil) (make-instance (quote voice) :tree (quote (1 (((4 4) (1 1 1 1))))) :chords (load-obj-list-from-save (quote ((let ((thechord (make-instance (quote chord) :lmidic (quote (6000)) :ldur (quote (1000)) :lvel (quote (100)) :loffset (quote (0)) :lchan (quote (1)) :approx (quote 2)))) (load